> What strategies, techniques, and tools are out there for this? I assume
> that a lot of the old knowledge applies - but probably there are some
> new ideas now that highly concurrent work is available at the personal
> level.

The old knowledge that still holds:

- GTD: capture everything into one inbox, decide each item's next
  action, and review the lists weekly. Its point is that you trust
  the system, so you stop holding the backlog in your head. Several
  capture inboxes defeat that, which is the trouble with the six
  files above.
- Kanban: make the stages visible and cap WIP at each one. Little's
  law says lead time is WIP divided by throughput, so if you start
  more work than you finish, each item takes longer, no matter how
  fast the workers are.
- How mailing-list projects track work. Git's maintainer posts a
  periodic "What's cooking" message that lists each topic branch and
  its state (new, in next, will merge, on hold). Patchwork and
  lore+b4 track the state of each patch for the kernel. Neither keeps
  a separate tracker. State lives in the list and the branches, and
  the report is generated from them. That matches the rule from [1].

What changes with agents. This part is my inference from how this
repo works, not something I found settled anywhere:

- The bottleneck moves. Agents make starting and doing work cheap,
  but you still review and integrate every result yourself. So the
  WIP limit that matters is on the review stage, i.e. how many
  series wait on you, not how many agents are running. Spare agent
  capacity is better spent on making each result cheaper to review
  (smaller patches, better messages, triage) than on starting more
  work.
- The task becomes an artifact. A clear task description is now
  close to the prompt itself. That supports zib/zib.org's idea:
  once work starts, the description moves into the change or thread
  that does it, and the backlog only holds what nobody has started.
- Status must be readable from outside. This is the same rule as
  "Own the loop" in CLAUDE.md. An item's state is wherever its
  real artifact is: no thread yet, a posted series, an Approved:
  trailer, a commit on trunk. A status field someone has to keep in
  sync will drift once several agents touch the same item.
- Tools built for this. A few git-backed trackers aimed at agents
  have appeared, e.g. Steve Yegge's beads (issues kept as JSONL in
  the repo, with dependencies and a "ready" query) and Backlog.md
  (one markdown file per task). Spec-driven tools such as GitHub's
  Spec Kit turn a written spec into a task list. I have not tried
  any of these here. Each one adds a second source of truth next to
  the list, which goes against [1], so I would borrow ideas from
  them rather than adopt them.

Here is what I recommend, in order:

1. One capture point. Merge TODO.md, doc/tasks.org and the duplicate
   fieldstones file into todo.org, as the draft plan proposes. Only
   work that nobody has started lives there.
2. Hand off by link. When an item starts, it becomes a list thread
   (an RFC or a series) and its heading gets the thread's id. At that
   point the list owns its state, and when the work lands the heading
   is deleted. You could set TODO and NEXT keywords on the headings
   for an org agenda, but nothing past NEXT belongs in the file.
3. Generate the WIP view, don't maintain it. A "What's cooking"
   report built from notmuch tags and jj (open series, approved but
   not applied, on b/dev, waiting on you) covers WIP. It is close to
   what the patch-triage skill already does, so it could grow from
   that.

Your sister RFC, "[RFC] push vs pull for agents" [3], is the other
half of this: if agents pull from the list, then the list is the
queue, and todo.org feeds it.

